%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: innovation in TE
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: What is the core idea behind the "Flipped Classroom" model in teacher
      education?
    Options:
    - Eliminating all face-to-face interaction.
    - Students review theoretical content online beforehand, using class time for
      active learning.
    - Teachers deliver lectures online only.
    - Focusing solely on practical skills in class.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which model involves a student-teacher working intensively for
      a full year alongside an expert mentor teacher in a school?
    Options:
    - The Traditional Apprenticeship Model
    - The Micro-teaching Model
    - The Correspondence Model
    - The Residency Model
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The use of Virtual Reality (VR) in teacher education is innovative
      because it allows for:'
    Options:
    - The complete replacement of real classroom experience.
    - Low-risk practice of teaching skills in a simulated environment.
    - Eliminating the need for a mentor.
    - Focusing only on content knowledge.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'An "Inquiry-Oriented" teacher education program positions the
      student-teacher as a(n):'
    Options:
    - Passive recipient of knowledge.
    - Active inquirer and co-constructor of knowledge.
    - Neutral observer.
    - Sole authority in the classroom.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: The National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 strongly recommends which
      innovative program structure?
    Options:
    - 1-year B.Ed. after post-graduation
    - Correspondence courses for teacher training
    - 2-year Diploma in Education
    - 4-year Integrated Teacher Education Program (ITEP)
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'A digital portfolio (e-portfolio) is primarily used for:'
    Options:
    - Storing administrative documents.
    - Showcasing and reflecting on a student-teacher's growth and competencies over
      time.
    - Replacing all formal assessments.
    - Communicating with parents.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'Problem-Based Learning (PBL) in teacher education typically begins
      with:'
    Options:
    - A lecture from an expert.
    - A final examination.
    - A real-world, complex educational problem.
    - A textbook chapter.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The primary goal of integrating "Social-Emotional Learning" (SEL)
      into teacher education is to:'
    Options:
    - Replace academic subject knowledge.
    - Equip teachers to foster their own and their students' emotional intelligence
      and well-being.
    - Focus solely on student discipline.
    - Reduce the curriculum load.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is an example of a performance-based assessment?
    Options:
    - A multiple-choice test on educational psychology.
    - A written essay on teaching philosophy.
    - A demonstration of a teaching skill followed by a viva voce.
    - A true/false quiz on child development.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: '"Blended Learning" in teacher education refers to a combination
      of:'
    Options:
    - Theory and practice.
    - Face-to-face and online learning experiences.
    - Primary and secondary education methods.
    - Indian and Western educational philosophies.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The innovation of "School-University Partnerships" aims to:'
    Options:
    - Give universities complete control over school curricula.
    - Create a collaborative ecosystem for mutual benefit and better teacher preparation.
    - Replace university-based teacher educators with school teachers.
    - Focus only on school infrastructure development.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'Using AI-powered analytics to provide feedback on a student-teacher''s
      questioning techniques is an innovation in:'
    Options:
    - Curriculum design.
    - Micro-teaching.
    - Admission procedures.
    - Infrastructure planning.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a key characteristic of an innovative
      teacher education program?
    Options:
    - A rigid, fixed curriculum.
    - A focus on rote memorization of theories.
    - Cultivating reflective and agentic teachers.
    - Isolation from technological tools.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "Case-Based Learning" approach uses:'
    Options:
    - Fictional stories for entertainment.
    - Detailed narratives of real classroom situations for analysis and problem-solving.
    - Only legal case studies.
    - Isolated facts and figures.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'Innovation in teacher education is necessary to:'
    Options:
    - Maintain the status quo.
    - Prepare teachers for the dynamic and complex 21st-century classroom.
    - Simplify the process of becoming a teacher.
    - Reduce the cost of teacher training.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "I" in the acronym "ITEP" stands for:'
    Options:
    - Indian
    - Innovative
    - Integrated
    - International
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'A "360-degree feedback" system for a student-teacher would include
      input from:'
    Options:
    - The student-teacher only.
    - The university supervisor only.
    - Multiple sources like the mentor teacher, peers, and supervisor.
    - Parents of school students.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which technological platform is widely used in India for teacher
      professional development?
    Options:
    - DIKSHA
    - NPTEL
    - IRCTC
    - MyGov
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'An innovative focus on "Inclusive Education" in teacher preparation
      ensures that teachers are ready to:'
    Options:
    - Teach only gifted students.
    - Cater to the diverse learning needs of all children.
    - Work only in private schools.
    - Follow a one-size-fits-all approach.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The shift from a "teacher-centered" to a "student-centered" approach
      is a fundamental:'
    Options:
    - Pedagogical innovation.
    - Administrative change.
    - Financial restructuring.
    - Political decision.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "T" in "ITEP" stands for:'
    Options:
    - Teaching
    - Training
    - Teacher
    - Technical
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'Using online forums for student-teachers to discuss and debate
      educational issues is an example of:'
    Options:
    - Isolated learning.
    - Blended learning and collaborative knowledge construction.
    - A waste of time.
    - Avoiding classroom interaction.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "E" in "ITEP" stands for:'
    Options:
    - Elementary
    - Education
    - Excellence
    - Environmental
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'An innovative assessment method that tracks growth over time is
      the:'
    Options:
    - Final written exam.
    - Portfolio assessment.
    - Pop quiz.
    - Viva voce.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "P" in "ITEP" stands for:'
    Options:
    - Primary
    - Professional
    - Practical
    - Program
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is an example of a 21st-century skill that
      should be explicitly taught in teacher education?
    Options:
    - Rote memorization
    - Digital literacy and collaboration
    - Calligraphy
    - Strict discipline enforcement
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'A key challenge in implementing technological innovations like
      VR in teacher education is:'
    Options:
    - Lack of student interest.
    - High cost and infrastructure requirements.
    - Over-qualification of teachers.
    - It being too easy to implement.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The innovation of "Reflective Practice" encourages teachers to:'
    Options:
    - Blame students for failures.
    - Avoid new teaching methods.
    - Follow the textbook without question.
    - Systematically analyze their own teaching to improve.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a structural innovation?
    Options:
    - Using a new textbook.
    - "Implementing a 4-year integrated\_B.Sc.\_B.Ed. program."
    - Changing the classroom seating arrangement.
    - Giving a PowerPoint presentation.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The ultimate aim of innovation in teacher education is to:'
    Options:
    - Make programs more complicated.
    - Improve the quality of learning for school students.
    - Increase the workload of teacher educators.
    - Attract more candidates regardless of quality.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: '"Gamification" in teacher education involves using:'
    Options:
    - Only video games for entertainment.
    - Game-design elements (like points, badges) to enhance motivation and engagement
      in learning.
    - Eliminating all forms of play.
    - Focusing only on sports.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'A "Professional Development School" is an example of a strong:'
    Options:
    - School-University Partnership.
    - Government Policy.
    - Student Union.
    - Corporate Sponsor.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'Innovation requires teacher educators to be:'
    Options:
    - Resistant to change.
    - Lifelong learners and innovators themselves.
    - Isolated from technology.
    - Focused only on traditional methods.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a pedagogical innovation?
    Options:
    - Building a new library.
    - Adopting a Problem-Based Learning (PBL) approach.
    - Increasing tuition fees.
    - Hiring more administrative staff.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The use of "Open Educational Resources (OER)" is an innovation
      that promotes:'
    Options:
    - Costly and copyrighted materials.
    - Traditional textbooks only.
    - Closed online platforms.
    - Free and accessible knowledge for teacher learning.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'A major barrier to innovation in teacher education in India is:'
    Options:
    - Too much funding for research.
    - Rigid regulatory frameworks and resistance to change.
    - An overabundance of innovative TEIs.
    - Lack of any educational policies.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "Flipped Classroom" model was made more feasible by the advent
      of:'
    Options:
    - Blackboards.
    - Digital video and online content platforms.
    - Overhead projectors.
    - Radio broadcasts.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'An innovative program that focuses on preparing teachers for rural
      and tribal areas would emphasize:'
    Options:
    - Only international curricula.
    - Context-specific pedagogy and community engagement.
    - Urban teaching methods only.
    - Avoiding local languages.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: '"Design Thinking" in teacher education encourages:'
    Options:
    - A one-size-fits-all solution.
    - A human-centered, iterative approach to solving educational problems.
    - Copying existing designs.
    - Avoiding any design process.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The success of any innovation in teacher education depends most
      critically on:'
    Options:
    - The color of the classroom walls.
    - The willingness and capacity of teacher educators and institutions to embrace
      change.
    - The weather.
    - The political party in power.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: '"Micro-credentials" or "Digital Badges" are an innovation in:'
    Options:
    - Certifying specific, discrete skills of teachers.
    - Replacing university degrees.
    - Awarding attendance.
    - Grading student assignments.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'An "Innovation Lab" or "Maker Space" in a Teacher Education Institution
      would be a place for student-teachers to:'
    Options:
    - Only take theoretical exams.
    - Tinker, create, and prototype new teaching-learning materials and strategies.
    - Isolate themselves.
    - Avoid practical work.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "E" in "OER" stands for:'
    Options:
    - Excellent
    - Educational
    - Electronic
    - Eastern
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "R" in "OER" stands for:'
    Options:
    - Resources
    - Research
    - Regulation
    - Reform
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a technological innovation?
    Options:
    - A new theory of learning.
    - Using a simulation to practice parent-teacher meetings.
    - Changing the semester schedule.
    - A new code of ethics for teachers.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'A key benefit of the "Integrated ITEP" model is that it:'
    Options:
    - Shortens the duration of teacher education.
    - Separates subject knowledge from pedagogical training.
    - Weaves together disciplinary knowledge and professional understanding from
      the beginning.
    - Focuses only on pedagogy.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'Innovation in assessment helps to create a culture of:'
    Options:
    - Fear and competition.
    - Learning and growth.
    - One-time performance.
    - Standardization only.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The "O" in "OER" stands for:'
    Options:
    - Official
    - Operational
    - Online
    - Open
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'A future-oriented innovation in teacher education is preparing
      teachers for:'
    Options:
    - A world without technology.
    - The ethical integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in education.
    - Using only chalk and talk methods.
    - Teaching a fixed, unchanging curriculum.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: innovation in TE
  - QuestionText: 'The spirit of innovation is best captured by a mindset of:'
    Options:
    - '"This is how it has always been done."'
    - '"New ideas are unnecessary."'
    - '"Change is too difficult."'
    - '"How can we do this better to meet the needs of future learners?"'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: innovation in TE
